window.PLAN=[{"id":"first-10-minutes","title":"First 10 minutes","icon":"zap-off","html":"\n<p>If the lights and Wi-Fi go out, do these in order. Print this page and tape it inside a kitchen cabinet; a plan on a dead phone is no plan.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check your breaker panel and look outside. If only your home is dark, it may be a tripped breaker (in an apartment, call the leasing office's emergency line). If the street is dark, it's an outage.</li>\n<li>Report it to your electric company. Their number is on the Call list and the home screen.</li>\n<li>Turn on a battery radio. In Austin, KLBJ 590 AM / 99.7 FM is the city's emergency-alert station.</li>\n<li>Put phones in low-power mode. Charge from a power bank, not the car, unless the car is outside with the garage door open.</li>\n<li>Unplug TVs, computers and other electronics to avoid a surge when power returns. Leave one lamp switched on so you know when it's back.</li>\n<li>Keep the fridge and freezer closed. Note the time the power went out.</li>\n<li>Fill the bathtub and a few pots with tap water right away. Upper floors lose water pressure first, and in Feb 2021 Austin lost water after power failed at its largest treatment plant.</li>\n<li>Gather everyone in one room. Get flashlights out; no candles near kids.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Key numbers</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Life-threatening emergency</td>\n<td>911</td>\n<td>Call; if calls fail, text 911</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Report power outage</td>\n<td>Your electric company</td>\n<td>See the Call list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social services, shelters, food, housing</td>\n<td>2-1-1 Texas</td>\n<td>211</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poison or carbon monoxide exposure</td>\n<td>Poison Control</td>\n<td>1-800-222-1222</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>City info and shelter rides (Austin)</td>\n<td>Austin 311</td>\n<td>311 or 512-974-2000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical equipment that needs power</td>\n<td>Texas STEAR registry</td>\n<td>Register by calling 211</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>Radio</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Station</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>What it carries</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>KLBJ (Austin)</td>\n<td>590 AM / 99.7 FM</td>\n<td>City of Austin emergency alerts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NOAA Weather Radio WXK27 (Austin)</td>\n<td>162.400 MHz</td>\n<td>Weather warnings 24/7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KUT (NPR)</td>\n<td>90.5 FM</td>\n<td>Local news</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","text":"If the lights and Wi-Fi go out, do these in order. Print this page and tape it inside a kitchen cabinet; a plan on a dead phone is no plan.\nCheck your breaker panel and look outside. If only your home is dark, it may be a tripped breaker (in an apartment, call the leasing office's emergency line). If the street is dark, it's an outage.\nReport it to your electric company. Their number is on the Call list and the home screen.\nTurn on a battery radio. In Austin, KLBJ 590 AM / 99.7 FM is the city's emergency-alert station.\nPut phones in low-power mode. Charge from a power bank, not the car, unless the car is outside with the garage door open.\nUnplug TVs, computers and other electronics to avoid a surge when power returns. Leave one lamp switched on so you know when it's back.\nKeep the fridge and freezer closed. Note the time the power went out.\nFill the bathtub and a few pots with tap water right away. Upper floors lose water pressure first, and in Feb 2021 Austin lost water after power failed at its largest treatment plant.\nGather everyone in one room. Get flashlights out; no candles near kids.\nKey numbers\nNeed\nWho\nHow\nLife-threatening emergency\n911\nCall; if calls fail, text 911\nReport power outage\nYour electric company\nSee the Call list\nSocial services, shelters, food, housing\n2-1-1 Texas\n211\nPoison or carbon monoxide exposure\nPoison Control\n1-800-222-1222\nCity info and shelter rides (Austin)\nAustin 311\n311 or 512-974-2000\nMedical equipment that needs power\nTexas STEAR registry\nRegister by calling 211\nRadio\nStation\nFrequency\nWhat it carries\nKLBJ (Austin)\n590 AM / 99.7 FM\nCity of Austin emergency alerts\nNOAA Weather Radio WXK27 (Austin)\n162.400 MHz\nWeather warnings 24/7\nKUT (NPR)\n90.5 FM\nLocal news"},{"id":"phased-action-plan","title":"Phased action plan","icon":"list-checks","html":"\n<p>Most outages are short, but Feb 2021 ran for days, so plan as if this one will too. Each phase adds to the one before it.</p>\n<h3>Phase 1: Hour 0 to 24 (stay home, conserve)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm the outage on the radio; listen at the top of each hour for scope and expected restore time.</li>\n<li>Set phones to low power. Pick ONE phone to stay on for news; others off except at set check-in times (for example 9 am, 1 pm, 6 pm).</li>\n<li>Text, don't call. Texts get through when networks are jammed. Send one group text to family with your status.</li>\n<li>Eat from the fridge first, then freezer, then shelf-stable food.</li>\n<li>Cold weather: move everyone to one interior room, close doors to unused rooms, hang blankets over windows.</li>\n<li>Hot weather: upper floors heat up first. Close blinds on sunny sides, open windows only at night.</li>\n<li>Check on the neighbors on each side, especially anyone elderly or with babies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase 2: Days 1 to 3 (manage food, water, temperature)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Fridge food has passed its 4-hour safe window; keep only what stayed at 4\u00b0C or below. A full freezer holds about 48 hours closed.</li>\n<li>Ration drinking water at 1 gallon per person per day; use tub water for flushing and washing.</li>\n<li>Watch for a boil-water notice on the radio. If one is issued, boil or treat all tap water (Water section).</li>\n<li>Charge devices from power banks or solar; recharge banks from the car only with the car outdoors.</li>\n<li>Keep a simple log on paper: indoor temperature morning and night, water left, food left.</li>\n<li>Decide each evening: is home still safe for everyone tonight? The Stay or go screen helps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase 3: Days 4 to 14 (sustain or relocate)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If roads are open, go to a library or rec center serving as a warming/cooling and charging center during its hours.</li>\n<li>Restock when stores reopen; stores on generators often open with cash-only or limited hours. Bring cash.</li>\n<li>Sanitation: if toilets can't flush, use the bucket-and-bag method (Water section).</li>\n<li>Rotate sleep so one adult is awake on cold nights to watch the kids and any heat source.</li>\n<li>Pool resources with neighbors: one person's camp stove, another's power station, shared watch.</li>\n<li>Plan relocation to family or a hotel outside the outage zone if indoor temperatures or water become unsafe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase 4: Beyond 2 weeks (long-term grid down)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Relocate if at all possible. A home without power, water and heat is not a safe place for young kids for weeks.</li>\n<li>If you must stay, join or organize a neighborhood group: shared cooking, water collection, a rota for checking on vulnerable neighbors.</li>\n<li>Register needs with 211 (and 311 in Austin); shelters and supply points are announced on local radio.</li>\n<li>Refill prescriptions the moment any pharmacy opens.</li>\n<li>Stay with your group; don't travel alone at night when streetlights and signals are out.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Most outages are short, but Feb 2021 ran for days, so plan as if this one will too. Each phase adds to the one before it.\nPhase 1: Hour 0 to 24 (stay home, conserve)\nConfirm the outage on the radio; listen at the top of each hour for scope and expected restore time.\nSet phones to low power. Pick ONE phone to stay on for news; others off except at set check-in times (for example 9 am, 1 pm, 6 pm).\nText, don't call. Texts get through when networks are jammed. Send one group text to family with your status.\nEat from the fridge first, then freezer, then shelf-stable food.\nCold weather: move everyone to one interior room, close doors to unused rooms, hang blankets over windows.\nHot weather: upper floors heat up first. Close blinds on sunny sides, open windows only at night.\nCheck on the neighbors on each side, especially anyone elderly or with babies.\nPhase 2: Days 1 to 3 (manage food, water, temperature)\nFridge food has passed its 4-hour safe window; keep only what stayed at 4\u00b0C or below. A full freezer holds about 48 hours closed.\nRation drinking water at 1 gallon per person per day; use tub water for flushing and washing.\nWatch for a boil-water notice on the radio. If one is issued, boil or treat all tap water (Water section).\nCharge devices from power banks or solar; recharge banks from the car only with the car outdoors.\nKeep a simple log on paper: indoor temperature morning and night, water left, food left.\nDecide each evening: is home still safe for everyone tonight? The Stay or go screen helps.\nPhase 3: Days 4 to 14 (sustain or relocate)\nIf roads are open, go to a library or rec center serving as a warming/cooling and charging center during its hours.\nRestock when stores reopen; stores on generators often open with cash-only or limited hours. Bring cash.\nSanitation: if toilets can't flush, use the bucket-and-bag method (Water section).\nRotate sleep so one adult is awake on cold nights to watch the kids and any heat source.\nPool resources with neighbors: one person's camp stove, another's power station, shared watch.\nPlan relocation to family or a hotel outside the outage zone if indoor temperatures or water become unsafe.\nPhase 4: Beyond 2 weeks (long-term grid down)\nRelocate if at all possible. A home without power, water and heat is not a safe place for young kids for weeks.\nIf you must stay, join or organize a neighborhood group: shared cooking, water collection, a rota for checking on vulnerable neighbors.\nRegister needs with 211 (and 311 in Austin); shelters and supply points are announced on local radio.\nRefill prescriptions the moment any pharmacy opens.\nStay with your group; don't travel alone at night when streetlights and signals are out."},{"id":"heat-cold-and-cooking-without-power","title":"Heat, cold and cooking without power","icon":"thermometer","html":"\n<p>When the power goes out, an all-electric home loses heat, cooling and cooking at once, and a gas home may lose heat too, since most furnaces need electricity. The biggest danger is not the cold itself but unsafe heaters and stoves brought indoors: carbon monoxide kills families every Texas freeze.</p>\n<h3>Never indoors, not even in the garage</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Generators, charcoal or propane grills, camp stoves meant for outdoors, car engines, patio heaters.</li>\n<li>A gas oven or stovetop for heat. It fills the room with carbon monoxide.</li>\n<li>Generators: run them at least 20 feet from the house with exhaust pointed away from doors and windows. Most apartment leases ban them; use a portable power station instead.</li>\n<li>Put a battery carbon monoxide alarm in the sleeping room. Buy one this week if you don't have one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Staying warm (freeze, like Feb 2021)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Pick the room with the fewest exterior walls and windows, usually a bedroom or the area by an interior hallway. Everyone sleeps there.</li>\n<li>Close doors to every other room. Roll towels against door gaps.</li>\n<li>Cover windows with blankets, quilts or cardboard; close blinds and curtains.</li>\n<li>Build a \"tent\" inside the room: a table or bunk covered with blankets, kids and adults inside together. Body heat in a small space adds up.</li>\n<li>Dress in layers: thermal base, fleece, coat, hat, wool socks. Change out of damp clothes immediately.</li>\n<li>Sleep on insulation, not the bare floor: sleeping pads, couch cushions, folded blankets under you.</li>\n<li>Eat and drink warm when you can. Calories produce heat; dehydration speeds hypothermia.</li>\n<li>Indoor-safe heat: a portable power station running a small electric blanket, hand warmers, hot water bottles filled from a pot heated outdoors.</li>\n<li>Protect pipes: open cabinet doors under sinks, let one faucet drip if the city hasn't asked you to conserve. Know where your main water shutoff is in case a pipe bursts.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Staying cool (summer outage, often over 38\u00b0C)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Close blinds and curtains on sunny windows by 9 am. Upper floors get hottest; in the hottest hours, move the kids to the lowest, shadiest spot.</li>\n<li>Open windows only at night when it's cooler outside than inside; close them again in the morning.</li>\n<li>Wet cloths on the neck, wrists and feet; battery fans moving air across damp skin.</li>\n<li>Drink water on a schedule, not just when thirsty. Toddlers dehydrate fastest.</li>\n<li>In the hottest hours, go to a public cooling center (libraries and rec centers during open hours), or sit in a running car in an open driveway with A/C on.</li>\n<li>Never leave a child in a parked car, even for a minute.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Cooking without power</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No-cook food</td>\n<td>Anywhere</td>\n<td>Canned meats and beans, peanut butter, crackers, cereal, fruit cups. Simplest and safest.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Butane camp stove</td>\n<td>Outdoors or in a well-ventilated area, per its label</td>\n<td>Store fuel canisters outside living areas.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Propane camp stove or grill</td>\n<td>Outdoors only, 10+ ft from the building. Fire code generally bars open-flame grills on apartment balconies</td>\n<td>Most reliable for boiling water. Keep 2 spare cylinders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Charcoal grill</td>\n<td>Outdoors only</td>\n<td>Never bring smoldering coals inside to \"warm the room.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portable power station + kettle or hot plate</td>\n<td>Indoors</td>\n<td>Drains a battery fast: boil small amounts only.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","text":"When the power goes out, an all-electric home loses heat, cooling and cooking at once, and a gas home may lose heat too, since most furnaces need electricity. The biggest danger is not the cold itself but unsafe heaters and stoves brought indoors: carbon monoxide kills families every Texas freeze.\nNever indoors, not even in the garage\nGenerators, charcoal or propane grills, camp stoves meant for outdoors, car engines, patio heaters.\nA gas oven or stovetop for heat. It fills the room with carbon monoxide.\nGenerators: run them at least 20 feet from the house with exhaust pointed away from doors and windows. Most apartment leases ban them; use a portable power station instead.\nPut a battery carbon monoxide alarm in the sleeping room. Buy one this week if you don't have one.\nStaying warm (freeze, like Feb 2021)\nPick the room with the fewest exterior walls and windows, usually a bedroom or the area by an interior hallway. Everyone sleeps there.\nClose doors to every other room. Roll towels against door gaps.\nCover windows with blankets, quilts or cardboard; close blinds and curtains.\nBuild a \"tent\" inside the room: a table or bunk covered with blankets, kids and adults inside together. Body heat in a small space adds up.\nDress in layers: thermal base, fleece, coat, hat, wool socks. Change out of damp clothes immediately.\nSleep on insulation, not the bare floor: sleeping pads, couch cushions, folded blankets under you.\nEat and drink warm when you can. Calories produce heat; dehydration speeds hypothermia.\nIndoor-safe heat: a portable power station running a small electric blanket, hand warmers, hot water bottles filled from a pot heated outdoors.\nProtect pipes: open cabinet doors under sinks, let one faucet drip if the city hasn't asked you to conserve. Know where your main water shutoff is in case a pipe bursts.\nStaying cool (summer outage, often over 38\u00b0C)\nClose blinds and curtains on sunny windows by 9 am. Upper floors get hottest; in the hottest hours, move the kids to the lowest, shadiest spot.\nOpen windows only at night when it's cooler outside than inside; close them again in the morning.\nWet cloths on the neck, wrists and feet; battery fans moving air across damp skin.\nDrink water on a schedule, not just when thirsty. Toddlers dehydrate fastest.\nIn the hottest hours, go to a public cooling center (libraries and rec centers during open hours), or sit in a running car in an open driveway with A/C on.\nNever leave a child in a parked car, even for a minute.\nCooking without power\nMethod\nWhere\nNotes\nNo-cook food\nAnywhere\nCanned meats and beans, peanut butter, crackers, cereal, fruit cups. Simplest and safest.\nButane camp stove\nOutdoors or in a well-ventilated area, per its label\nStore fuel canisters outside living areas.\nPropane camp stove or grill\nOutdoors only, 10+ ft from the building. Fire code generally bars open-flame grills on apartment balconies\nMost reliable for boiling water. Keep 2 spare cylinders.\nCharcoal grill\nOutdoors only\nNever bring smoldering coals inside to \"warm the room.\"\nPortable power station + kettle or hot plate\nIndoors\nDrains a battery fast: boil small amounts only."},{"id":"water-and-food","title":"Water and food","icon":"droplets","html":"\n<p>Store 1 gallon per person per day for 14 days. The Supplies screen works out your family's total. Stackable 5-gallon containers under beds and in closets, plus a bathtub water bladder (about 100 gallons, filled at the start of an outage), make that fit. Tap water depends on power at treatment plants: in Feb 2021 a power loss at Austin's Ullrich plant triggered a citywide boil-water notice that lasted about a week.</p>\n<h3>Water sources, in order of use</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Stored bottled water and filled containers (drinking, kids' cups, cooking).</li>\n<li>Bathtub and pots filled at the start of the outage (flushing, washing).</li>\n<li>Your water heater tank, typically 40 to 50 gallons. Turn off its breaker (or gas) first, let it cool, then drain from the valve at the bottom. Turn it back on only after the tank is refilled.</li>\n<li>Ice in the freezer as it melts.</li>\n<li>Never: pool, creek or rainwater for drinking unless boiled or filtered and treated.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Making water safe</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>During a boil-water notice, bring water to a rolling boil for 2 minutes, then cool before use.</li>\n<li>No way to boil: use unscented household bleach. Per gallon of clear water, 8 drops of 6% bleach or 6 drops of 8.25% bleach. Stir, wait 30 minutes; it should smell faintly of chlorine. Double it for cloudy water, and filter cloudy water through a cloth first.</li>\n<li>Kids' cups: use bottled water or water you've boiled. Shelf-stable milk boxes need no water or refrigeration; keep a supply.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Food safety</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Safe window, door closed</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Refrigerator</td>\n<td>About 4 hours</td>\n<td>Throw out milk, meat, leftovers, cut fruit that sat above 4\u00b0C for 2+ hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full freezer</td>\n<td>About 48 hours</td>\n<td>Food with ice crystals or at 4\u00b0C or below can be refrozen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Half-full freezer</td>\n<td>About 24 hours</td>\n<td>Pack items together; add bags of ice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breast milk (fridge)</td>\n<td>Use within the fridge's 4-hour window</td>\n<td>Move to a cooler with ice right away</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>When in doubt, throw it out. Keep a fridge thermometer inside so you're not guessing. In a winter outage you can store food in a cooler outdoors in shade, if it stays below 4\u00b0C and away from animals.</p>\n<h3>3-day no-cook menu</h3>\n<p>Use this as the shopping list for the first 3 days; repeat or rotate it for 14. Buy foods your kids already eat, and pack a manual can opener.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Breakfast</th>\n<th>Lunch</th>\n<th>Dinner</th>\n<th>Kids' snacks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Dry cereal with shelf-stable milk boxes, bananas</td>\n<td>Peanut butter on crackers, applesauce pouches</td>\n<td>Canned chicken with crackers, canned corn, fruit cups</td>\n<td>Cheese sticks from the fridge (eat first), raisins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Granola bars, shelf-stable milk, canned peaches</td>\n<td>Tuna or chicken salad kits, pretzels, baby carrots (if still cold)</td>\n<td>Canned beans and rice cups (eaten room-temperature), canned green beans</td>\n<td>Fruit pouches, animal crackers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Instant oatmeal with hot water from the camp stove, or cereal</td>\n<td>Peanut butter and jelly on shelf-stable tortillas</td>\n<td>Canned ravioli or chili (heated outdoors if possible), applesauce</td>\n<td>Crackers, dried fruit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>For kids under 4, cut anything round or firm into small pieces and skip whole nuts and popcorn.</p>\n<h3>Sanitation without water</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Toilets still flush if you pour a bucket of water (about 1 to 2 gallons) straight into the bowl.</li>\n<li>No water at all: line the toilet or a 5-gallon bucket with two heavy trash bags, add a scoop of kitty litter or sawdust after each use, tie off and store outside.</li>\n<li>Hand sanitizer before eating and after diapers or the toilet. Stomach illness spreads fast when washing is limited.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Store 1 gallon per person per day for 14 days. The Supplies screen works out your family's total. Stackable 5-gallon containers under beds and in closets, plus a bathtub water bladder (about 100 gallons, filled at the start of an outage), make that fit. Tap water depends on power at treatment plants: in Feb 2021 a power loss at Austin's Ullrich plant triggered a citywide boil-water notice that lasted about a week.\nWater sources, in order of use\nStored bottled water and filled containers (drinking, kids' cups, cooking).\nBathtub and pots filled at the start of the outage (flushing, washing).\nYour water heater tank, typically 40 to 50 gallons. Turn off its breaker (or gas) first, let it cool, then drain from the valve at the bottom. Turn it back on only after the tank is refilled.\nIce in the freezer as it melts.\nNever: pool, creek or rainwater for drinking unless boiled or filtered and treated.\nMaking water safe\nDuring a boil-water notice, bring water to a rolling boil for 2 minutes, then cool before use.\nNo way to boil: use unscented household bleach. Per gallon of clear water, 8 drops of 6% bleach or 6 drops of 8.25% bleach. Stir, wait 30 minutes; it should smell faintly of chlorine. Double it for cloudy water, and filter cloudy water through a cloth first.\nKids' cups: use bottled water or water you've boiled. Shelf-stable milk boxes need no water or refrigeration; keep a supply.\nFood safety\nItem\nSafe window, door closed\nRule\nRefrigerator\nAbout 4 hours\nThrow out milk, meat, leftovers, cut fruit that sat above 4\u00b0C for 2+ hours\nFull freezer\nAbout 48 hours\nFood with ice crystals or at 4\u00b0C or below can be refrozen\nHalf-full freezer\nAbout 24 hours\nPack items together; add bags of ice\nBreast milk (fridge)\nUse within the fridge's 4-hour window\nMove to a cooler with ice right away\nWhen in doubt, throw it out. Keep a fridge thermometer inside so you're not guessing. In a winter outage you can store food in a cooler outdoors in shade, if it stays below 4\u00b0C and away from animals.\n3-day no-cook menu\nUse this as the shopping list for the first 3 days; repeat or rotate it for 14. Buy foods your kids already eat, and pack a manual can opener.\nDay\nBreakfast\nLunch\nDinner\nKids' snacks\n1\nDry cereal with shelf-stable milk boxes, bananas\nPeanut butter on crackers, applesauce pouches\nCanned chicken with crackers, canned corn, fruit cups\nCheese sticks from the fridge (eat first), raisins\n2\nGranola bars, shelf-stable milk, canned peaches\nTuna or chicken salad kits, pretzels, baby carrots (if still cold)\nCanned beans and rice cups (eaten room-temperature), canned green beans\nFruit pouches, animal crackers\n3\nInstant oatmeal with hot water from the camp stove, or cereal\nPeanut butter and jelly on shelf-stable tortillas\nCanned ravioli or chili (heated outdoors if possible), applesauce\nCrackers, dried fruit\nFor kids under 4, cut anything round or firm into small pieces and skip whole nuts and popcorn.\nSanitation without water\nToilets still flush if you pour a bucket of water (about 1 to 2 gallons) straight into the bowl.\nNo water at all: line the toilet or a 5-gallon bucket with two heavy trash bags, add a scoop of kitty litter or sawdust after each use, tie off and store outside.\nHand sanitizer before eating and after diapers or the toilet. Stomach illness spreads fast when washing is limited."},{"id":"power-phones-and-communication","title":"Power, phones and communication","icon":"radio","html":"\n<p>When internet and cell service fail, the radio is your news and pre-agreed meeting points are your phone. Settle the plan below with everyone in the house before you need it.</p>\n<h3>Keeping devices alive</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Power banks: at least one 20,000 mAh bank per adult, kept charged. Each refills a phone roughly 3 to 4 times.</li>\n<li>A portable power station (500 Wh or more) can run a CPAP, a small fan, an electric blanket or a router for hours. Pair it with a folding solar panel for multi-day outages.</li>\n<li>Car charging: only with the car parked outside, never in a closed or open garage. Run the engine 30 minutes at a time and keep the tank at least half full from October through March.</li>\n<li>Turn off Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, drop screen brightness, close apps. Airplane mode except at check-in times.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Getting information with no internet</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Battery or hand-crank AM/FM/NOAA radio tuned to local news and NOAA weather.</li>\n<li>Phone emergency alerts (WEA) reach you over the cell network even when data is down. Keep them switched on.</li>\n<li>Before the next outage, register at WarnCentralTexas.org for text, call and email alerts tied to your home address.</li>\n<li>Sign up for your electric company's outage texts (see Call list).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Family communication plan</h3>\n<p>Fill these in on the Our details screen so they're on every phone, and write them on paper too.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Out-of-area contact everyone texts</td>\n<td>Long-distance texts often get through when local calls don't</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meeting point: home</td>\n<td>Where everyone heads if separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A spot right outside home</td>\n<td>For a fire or anything else that keeps you out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fallback 1: nearby place with power</td>\n<td>Friends or family across town</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fallback 2: out of town</td>\n<td>Family, friends or a hotel outside the outage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check-in times if phones work</td>\n<td>For example 9 am, 1 pm, 6 pm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Write these numbers on paper in every adult's wallet and every child's backpack. Young kids should know their full name, a parent's name and phone number, and the meeting point.</p>","text":"When internet and cell service fail, the radio is your news and pre-agreed meeting points are your phone. Settle the plan below with everyone in the house before you need it.\nKeeping devices alive\nPower banks: at least one 20,000 mAh bank per adult, kept charged. Each refills a phone roughly 3 to 4 times.\nA portable power station (500 Wh or more) can run a CPAP, a small fan, an electric blanket or a router for hours. Pair it with a folding solar panel for multi-day outages.\nCar charging: only with the car parked outside, never in a closed or open garage. Run the engine 30 minutes at a time and keep the tank at least half full from October through March.\nTurn off Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, drop screen brightness, close apps. Airplane mode except at check-in times.\nGetting information with no internet\nBattery or hand-crank AM/FM/NOAA radio tuned to local news and NOAA weather.\nPhone emergency alerts (WEA) reach you over the cell network even when data is down. Keep them switched on.\nBefore the next outage, register at WarnCentralTexas.org for text, call and email alerts tied to your home address.\nSign up for your electric company's outage texts (see Call list).\nFamily communication plan\nFill these in on the Our details screen so they're on every phone, and write them on paper too.\nItem\nWhy\nOut-of-area contact everyone texts\nLong-distance texts often get through when local calls don't\nMeeting point: home\nWhere everyone heads if separated\nA spot right outside home\nFor a fire or anything else that keeps you out\nFallback 1: nearby place with power\nFriends or family across town\nFallback 2: out of town\nFamily, friends or a hotel outside the outage\nCheck-in times if phones work\nFor example 9 am, 1 pm, 6 pm\nWrite these numbers on paper in every adult's wallet and every child's backpack. Young kids should know their full name, a parent's name and phone number, and the meeting point."},{"id":"medical-guide","title":"Medical guide","icon":"heart-pulse","html":"\n<p>This guide is first aid until help arrives, not a substitute for it. Know where your nearest 24-hour ER and pediatric ER are, and add them to Our details. A CPR and first-aid class (Red Cross or AHA) is the single best prep step in this plan.</p>\n<h3>Call 911 or go to the ER now</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Not breathing, blue lips, choking that back blows and chest thrusts don't clear</li>\n<li>Unconscious, confused, seizing, or can't be woken</li>\n<li>Suspected carbon monoxide poisoning (get everyone outside first)</li>\n<li>Bleeding that soaks through a pressure dressing</li>\n<li>A child with fever who is very hard to wake, has a stiff neck, trouble breathing, or a rash that doesn't fade when pressed</li>\n<li>Signs of heat stroke or severe hypothermia (below)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If 911 won't connect, text 911, or go to the nearest fire station; stations are staffed around the clock.</p>\n<h3>Emergencies an outage makes more likely</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Warning signs</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Carbon monoxide poisoning</td>\n<td>Headache, dizziness, nausea, sleepiness, several people or pets sick at once; better when away from home</td>\n<td>Everyone outside into fresh air immediately. Call 911 from outside. Don't go back in.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothermia</td>\n<td>Shivering, slurred speech, clumsiness, confusion; toddlers: cold red skin, unusual fussiness or very low energy. Shivering that stops is worse, not better</td>\n<td>Move to the warmest spot, remove wet clothes, warm the chest and core first with blankets and skin-to-skin contact, warm sweet drinks if fully alert. Call 911 if confused or drowsy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frostbite</td>\n<td>Numb, white or grayish-yellow, waxy skin on fingers, toes, ears, nose</td>\n<td>Warm gently in warm (not hot) water. Don't rub it. Don't rewarm if it may refreeze. Get care.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat exhaustion</td>\n<td>Heavy sweating, cool clammy skin, weakness, nausea, headache, fast pulse</td>\n<td>Cool place, loosen clothes, wet cloths, sips of water. Get care if vomiting or not better within an hour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat stroke</td>\n<td>Body temp 39.5\u00b0C or higher, hot red skin (dry or damp), confusion, fast strong pulse, passing out</td>\n<td>Call 911. Cool aggressively: cold water, ice packs at neck, armpits and groin. No fluids if not fully alert.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dehydration (kids)</td>\n<td>No wet diaper or potty trip in 8 hours, no tears when crying, dry mouth, unusually sleepy</td>\n<td>Small frequent sips of oral rehydration solution (Pedialyte), a sip every few minutes. Get care if no improvement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Burns</td>\n<td>Red, blistered or white/charred skin</td>\n<td>Cool running water 10 to 20 minutes (no ice, butter or ointment). Cover loosely with a clean dressing. Large burns, face, hands, or a child's burn: get care.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep cuts, heavy bleeding</td>\n<td>Blood pooling or spurting</td>\n<td>Firm direct pressure with gauze for 10 minutes without peeking. Add layers, don't remove. Tourniquet above the wound for limb bleeding you can't control.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Food poisoning</td>\n<td>Vomiting, diarrhea after eating questionable food</td>\n<td>Fluids, rest. Get care for blood in stool, high fever, or signs of dehydration.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>Medications to keep on hand</h3>\n<p>Stock a 2-week supply. Kids get children's formulas only: dose by your child's weight using the chart on that product's label and the measuring device in its box, never a cut-down adult dose. Ask your pediatrician for a printed dosing chart at the next visit and keep it with the kit.</p>\n<h4>For young kids</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Medicine</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Children's acetaminophen liquid (Tylenol)</td>\n<td>Fever, pain</td>\n<td>Check the concentration on the box matches your dosing chart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Children's ibuprofen liquid (Motrin, Advil)</td>\n<td>Fever, pain, swelling</td>\n<td>Not for babies under 6 months; give with food; don't use if vomiting or dehydrated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Children's allergy medicine labeled for your child's age</td>\n<td>Allergies, hives, itching</td>\n<td>Ask the pediatrician what to use for a sudden allergic reaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oral rehydration solution (Pedialyte or similar)</td>\n<td>Vomiting, diarrhea, heat</td>\n<td>Liquid bottles plus powder packets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saline nose drops and a nasal aspirator</td>\n<td>Stuffy nose, colds</td>\n<td>Children's cough and cold medicines are not recommended under age 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honey</td>\n<td>Cough (over age 1 only)</td>\n<td>A spoonful before bed for the cough</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diaper rash cream (zinc oxide)</td>\n<td>Rash</td>\n<td>For kids in diapers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hydrocortisone 1% cream</td>\n<td>Bug bites, itchy rashes</td>\n<td>Small amounts, short use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Children's sunscreen and insect repellent (picaridin or DEET)</td>\n<td>Outdoor time</td>\n<td>Mosquitoes increase when water stands after storms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>For the adults</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Medicine</th>\n<th>For</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Acetaminophen and ibuprofen (or naproxen)</td>\n<td>Pain, fever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Antihistamines: cetirizine or loratadine (non-drowsy), diphenhydramine</td>\n<td>Allergies, hives, sleep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loperamide (Imodium)</td>\n<td>Diarrhea (adults only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol)</td>\n<td>Upset stomach (adults only; not for children)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Antacids and famotidine</td>\n<td>Heartburn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meclizine</td>\n<td>Nausea, motion sickness on long drives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrolyte packets</td>\n<td>Heat, illness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lubricating eye drops, lip balm</td>\n<td>Dry air, smoke</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>For everyone</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Antibiotic ointment, hydrocortisone cream, burn gel, aloe</li>\n<li>Oral rehydration solution and electrolyte packets</li>\n<li>Each person's prescriptions: 2-week supply minimum, plus a written list of names, doses, prescribing doctor and pharmacy</li>\n<li>Any prescribed epinephrine auto-injector or inhaler: keep a spare in the go-bag</li>\n<li>Glasses or spare contacts and solution</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Storing medicines safely</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Lock everything in a box kept up high. During an outage, a toddler finding pills is a bigger danger than most illnesses; poison control is 1-800-222-1222.</li>\n<li>Keep medicines in the warm room in winter and out of a hot car in summer; heat ruins many liquids.</li>\n<li>Check expiration dates every 6 months (when the clocks change) and replace what's expired.</li>\n<li>Insulin and other refrigerated prescriptions go in a cooler with ice packs; check the package insert for how long they last at room temperature.</li>\n<li>If someone depends on powered medical equipment, register with the State of Texas Emergency Assistance Registry (STEAR) by calling 211.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>First-aid kit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Gauze pads and rolls, adhesive bandages in several sizes, medical tape</li>\n<li>Elastic bandage, triangular bandage, trauma shears</li>\n<li>Tourniquet (CAT or SOF-T style) and a pressure bandage</li>\n<li>Digital thermometer (ear or forehead types are easiest with toddlers)</li>\n<li>Children's and adult fever/pain relievers, with their measuring tools</li>\n<li>Oral rehydration solution packets or Pedialyte</li>\n<li>Antihistamine, hydrocortisone cream, antibiotic ointment</li>\n<li>Tweezers, saline wound wash, nitrile gloves, hand sanitizer</li>\n<li>Burn gel dressings, instant cold packs, hand warmers</li>\n<li>Emergency (mylar) blankets</li>\n<li>Copies of insurance cards and the medication list</li>\n</ul>","text":"This guide is first aid until help arrives, not a substitute for it. Know where your nearest 24-hour ER and pediatric ER are, and add them to Our details. A CPR and first-aid class (Red Cross or AHA) is the single best prep step in this plan.\nCall 911 or go to the ER now\nNot breathing, blue lips, choking that back blows and chest thrusts don't clear\nUnconscious, confused, seizing, or can't be woken\nSuspected carbon monoxide poisoning (get everyone outside first)\nBleeding that soaks through a pressure dressing\nA child with fever who is very hard to wake, has a stiff neck, trouble breathing, or a rash that doesn't fade when pressed\nSigns of heat stroke or severe hypothermia (below)\nIf 911 won't connect, text 911, or go to the nearest fire station; stations are staffed around the clock.\nEmergencies an outage makes more likely\nCondition\nWarning signs\nWhat to do\nCarbon monoxide poisoning\nHeadache, dizziness, nausea, sleepiness, several people or pets sick at once; better when away from home\nEveryone outside into fresh air immediately. Call 911 from outside. Don't go back in.\nHypothermia\nShivering, slurred speech, clumsiness, confusion; toddlers: cold red skin, unusual fussiness or very low energy. Shivering that stops is worse, not better\nMove to the warmest spot, remove wet clothes, warm the chest and core first with blankets and skin-to-skin contact, warm sweet drinks if fully alert. Call 911 if confused or drowsy.\nFrostbite\nNumb, white or grayish-yellow, waxy skin on fingers, toes, ears, nose\nWarm gently in warm (not hot) water. Don't rub it. Don't rewarm if it may refreeze. Get care.\nHeat exhaustion\nHeavy sweating, cool clammy skin, weakness, nausea, headache, fast pulse\nCool place, loosen clothes, wet cloths, sips of water. Get care if vomiting or not better within an hour.\nHeat stroke\nBody temp 39.5\u00b0C or higher, hot red skin (dry or damp), confusion, fast strong pulse, passing out\nCall 911. Cool aggressively: cold water, ice packs at neck, armpits and groin. No fluids if not fully alert.\nDehydration (kids)\nNo wet diaper or potty trip in 8 hours, no tears when crying, dry mouth, unusually sleepy\nSmall frequent sips of oral rehydration solution (Pedialyte), a sip every few minutes. Get care if no improvement.\nBurns\nRed, blistered or white/charred skin\nCool running water 10 to 20 minutes (no ice, butter or ointment). Cover loosely with a clean dressing. Large burns, face, hands, or a child's burn: get care.\nDeep cuts, heavy bleeding\nBlood pooling or spurting\nFirm direct pressure with gauze for 10 minutes without peeking. Add layers, don't remove. Tourniquet above the wound for limb bleeding you can't control.\nFood poisoning\nVomiting, diarrhea after eating questionable food\nFluids, rest. Get care for blood in stool, high fever, or signs of dehydration.\nMedications to keep on hand\nStock a 2-week supply. Kids get children's formulas only: dose by your child's weight using the chart on that product's label and the measuring device in its box, never a cut-down adult dose. Ask your pediatrician for a printed dosing chart at the next visit and keep it with the kit.\nFor young kids\nMedicine\nFor\nNotes\nChildren's acetaminophen liquid (Tylenol)\nFever, pain\nCheck the concentration on the box matches your dosing chart\nChildren's ibuprofen liquid (Motrin, Advil)\nFever, pain, swelling\nNot for babies under 6 months; give with food; don't use if vomiting or dehydrated\nChildren's allergy medicine labeled for your child's age\nAllergies, hives, itching\nAsk the pediatrician what to use for a sudden allergic reaction\nOral rehydration solution (Pedialyte or similar)\nVomiting, diarrhea, heat\nLiquid bottles plus powder packets\nSaline nose drops and a nasal aspirator\nStuffy nose, colds\nChildren's cough and cold medicines are not recommended under age 4\nHoney\nCough (over age 1 only)\nA spoonful before bed for the cough\nDiaper rash cream (zinc oxide)\nRash\nFor kids in diapers\nHydrocortisone 1% cream\nBug bites, itchy rashes\nSmall amounts, short use\nChildren's sunscreen and insect repellent (picaridin or DEET)\nOutdoor time\nMosquitoes increase when water stands after storms\nFor the adults\nMedicine\nFor\nAcetaminophen and ibuprofen (or naproxen)\nPain, fever\nAntihistamines: cetirizine or loratadine (non-drowsy), diphenhydramine\nAllergies, hives, sleep\nLoperamide (Imodium)\nDiarrhea (adults only)\nBismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol)\nUpset stomach (adults only; not for children)\nAntacids and famotidine\nHeartburn\nMeclizine\nNausea, motion sickness on long drives\nElectrolyte packets\nHeat, illness\nLubricating eye drops, lip balm\nDry air, smoke\nFor everyone\nAntibiotic ointment, hydrocortisone cream, burn gel, aloe\nOral rehydration solution and electrolyte packets\nEach person's prescriptions: 2-week supply minimum, plus a written list of names, doses, prescribing doctor and pharmacy\nAny prescribed epinephrine auto-injector or inhaler: keep a spare in the go-bag\nGlasses or spare contacts and solution\nStoring medicines safely\nLock everything in a box kept up high. During an outage, a toddler finding pills is a bigger danger than most illnesses; poison control is 1-800-222-1222.\nKeep medicines in the warm room in winter and out of a hot car in summer; heat ruins many liquids.\nCheck expiration dates every 6 months (when the clocks change) and replace what's expired.\nInsulin and other refrigerated prescriptions go in a cooler with ice packs; check the package insert for how long they last at room temperature.\nIf someone depends on powered medical equipment, register with the State of Texas Emergency Assistance Registry (STEAR) by calling 211.\nFirst-aid kit\nGauze pads and rolls, adhesive bandages in several sizes, medical tape\nElastic bandage, triangular bandage, trauma shears\nTourniquet (CAT or SOF-T style) and a pressure bandage\nDigital thermometer (ear or forehead types are easiest with toddlers)\nChildren's and adult fever/pain relievers, with their measuring tools\nOral rehydration solution packets or Pedialyte\nAntihistamine, hydrocortisone cream, antibiotic ointment\nTweezers, saline wound wash, nitrile gloves, hand sanitizer\nBurn gel dressings, instant cold packs, hand warmers\nEmergency (mylar) blankets\nCopies of insurance cards and the medication list"},{"id":"kids","title":"Kids","icon":"baby","html":"\n<p>Toddlers and young kids lose heat and fluids faster than adults and can't always tell you they feel sick, so the youngest child sets the household's limits: when home becomes unsafe for them, it's time to go.</p>\n<h3>Food and drink</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Two weeks of foods they already eat: shelf-stable milk boxes, fruit and applesauce pouches, crackers, dry cereal, peanut butter (if no allergy), granola bars, canned fruit.</li>\n<li>Choking risk under age 4: no whole nuts, popcorn or hard candy; cut grapes and hot-dog pieces lengthwise.</li>\n<li>Spill-proof cups filled with bottled or boiled water; offer a drink every hour, more in heat.</li>\n<li>Hungry, tired toddlers melt down fast: keep snack times on their usual schedule.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Warm and safe at night</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>One more layer than an adult: thermal top and bottoms, fleece pajamas, wool socks, a hat.</li>\n<li>Everyone sleeps together in the warm room. For babies, follow safe-sleep rules: their own firm, flat sleep space with no loose blankets; dress them warmly instead.</li>\n<li>Check the back of the neck or chest, not hands: cold red skin, unusual fussiness, clumsiness or sleepiness means they need warming.</li>\n<li>Keep kids well away from any heat source, candle or camp stove; a toddler gate across a doorway helps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Hot weather</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Light clothes, shade, cool damp washcloths, a battery fan.</li>\n<li>Offer water often; watch for peeing less than usual, the first sign of dehydration.</li>\n<li>Never leave a child in a parked car, even for a minute.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Diapers, potty and hygiene</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Kids in diapers or pull-ups: a 2-week supply (roughly 6 a day), wipes, rash cream, plastic bags.</li>\n<li>A portable potty with disposable liners works when toilets can't flush and keeps potty-trained kids on track.</li>\n<li>Hand sanitizer before every snack; stomach bugs spread fast through a family with little water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Keeping kids calm</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep meals, naps and bedtime on the usual schedule as much as you can.</li>\n<li>Give each child their own small flashlight or glow stick; no candles in reach.</li>\n<li>An outage box with books, crayons, cards, stickers, play-dough, puzzles and a comfort toy for each.</li>\n<li>Explain simply that the lights are off and the grown-ups have a plan. Kids take their cue from your tone. Kids Mode in this app has a story and games for exactly this.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Teach them now</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Ages 4 and up: full name, your full names, your phone number (the phone game in Kids Mode helps) and that the meeting point is home.</li>\n<li>Younger kids: their name and yours. Write your phone number inside their jacket or on a wristband.</li>\n<li>Everyone: if lost, stay put and find a police officer, firefighter or a mom with kids; never touch wires, generators, heaters or candles.</li>\n<li>During any emergency, take a full-length phone photo of each child every morning. If one gets separated, you'll have what they're wearing today.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Toddlers and young kids lose heat and fluids faster than adults and can't always tell you they feel sick, so the youngest child sets the household's limits: when home becomes unsafe for them, it's time to go.\nFood and drink\nTwo weeks of foods they already eat: shelf-stable milk boxes, fruit and applesauce pouches, crackers, dry cereal, peanut butter (if no allergy), granola bars, canned fruit.\nChoking risk under age 4: no whole nuts, popcorn or hard candy; cut grapes and hot-dog pieces lengthwise.\nSpill-proof cups filled with bottled or boiled water; offer a drink every hour, more in heat.\nHungry, tired toddlers melt down fast: keep snack times on their usual schedule.\nWarm and safe at night\nOne more layer than an adult: thermal top and bottoms, fleece pajamas, wool socks, a hat.\nEveryone sleeps together in the warm room. For babies, follow safe-sleep rules: their own firm, flat sleep space with no loose blankets; dress them warmly instead.\nCheck the back of the neck or chest, not hands: cold red skin, unusual fussiness, clumsiness or sleepiness means they need warming.\nKeep kids well away from any heat source, candle or camp stove; a toddler gate across a doorway helps.\nHot weather\nLight clothes, shade, cool damp washcloths, a battery fan.\nOffer water often; watch for peeing less than usual, the first sign of dehydration.\nNever leave a child in a parked car, even for a minute.\nDiapers, potty and hygiene\nKids in diapers or pull-ups: a 2-week supply (roughly 6 a day), wipes, rash cream, plastic bags.\nA portable potty with disposable liners works when toilets can't flush and keeps potty-trained kids on track.\nHand sanitizer before every snack; stomach bugs spread fast through a family with little water.\nKeeping kids calm\nKeep meals, naps and bedtime on the usual schedule as much as you can.\nGive each child their own small flashlight or glow stick; no candles in reach.\nAn outage box with books, crayons, cards, stickers, play-dough, puzzles and a comfort toy for each.\nExplain simply that the lights are off and the grown-ups have a plan. Kids take their cue from your tone. Kids Mode in this app has a story and games for exactly this.\nTeach them now\nAges 4 and up: full name, your full names, your phone number (the phone game in Kids Mode helps) and that the meeting point is home.\nYounger kids: their name and yours. Write your phone number inside their jacket or on a wristband.\nEveryone: if lost, stay put and find a police officer, firefighter or a mom with kids; never touch wires, generators, heaters or candles.\nDuring any emergency, take a full-length phone photo of each child every morning. If one gets separated, you'll have what they're wearing today."},{"id":"central-texas-hazards-that-cause-outages","title":"Central Texas hazards that cause outages","icon":"triangle-alert","html":"\n<p>Central Texas is one of the most flash-flood-prone regions in the country, and storms that knock out power often bring flooding, tornadoes or extreme heat with them.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hazard</th>\n<th>Warning</th>\n<th>What your family does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flash flood</td>\n<td>Flash Flood Warning on phone, radio or NOAA radio</td>\n<td>Never drive or walk into moving water: about 6 inches can knock an adult down and 12 inches can float a car. Turn around, don't drown. Check ATXfloods.com for closed low-water crossings before driving.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tornado</td>\n<td>Tornado Warning, outdoor sirens</td>\n<td>Go to the lowest floor, in an interior hallway or room away from windows. Upstairs in an apartment: get to a ground-floor interior stairwell or hallway if you can do it quickly. Shoes on, helmets or pillows over the kids' heads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extreme heat</td>\n<td>Heat Advisory, Excessive Heat Warning</td>\n<td>Follow the heat steps above; with no A/C, leave for a cooling center or Fallback 1 by early afternoon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wildfire</td>\n<td>Evacuation order, smoke</td>\n<td>Leave immediately when told; keep go-bags by the door during red-flag fire weather. Close windows and run a portable air purifier if smoke is heavy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ice storm, freeze</td>\n<td>Winter Storm Warning</td>\n<td>Expect power and water outages lasting days (Feb 2021, Feb 2023). Fill water, charge everything and fuel the car before it arrives.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","text":"Central Texas is one of the most flash-flood-prone regions in the country, and storms that knock out power often bring flooding, tornadoes or extreme heat with them.\nHazard\nWarning\nWhat your family does\nFlash flood\nFlash Flood Warning on phone, radio or NOAA radio\nNever drive or walk into moving water: about 6 inches can knock an adult down and 12 inches can float a car. Turn around, don't drown. Check ATXfloods.com for closed low-water crossings before driving.\nTornado\nTornado Warning, outdoor sirens\nGo to the lowest floor, in an interior hallway or room away from windows. Upstairs in an apartment: get to a ground-floor interior stairwell or hallway if you can do it quickly. Shoes on, helmets or pillows over the kids' heads.\nExtreme heat\nHeat Advisory, Excessive Heat Warning\nFollow the heat steps above; with no A/C, leave for a cooling center or Fallback 1 by early afternoon.\nWildfire\nEvacuation order, smoke\nLeave immediately when told; keep go-bags by the door during red-flag fire weather. Close windows and run a portable air purifier if smoke is heavy.\nIce storm, freeze\nWinter Storm Warning\nExpect power and water outages lasting days (Feb 2021, Feb 2023). Fill water, charge everything and fuel the car before it arrives."},{"id":"safety-security-and-evacuation","title":"Safety, security and evacuation","icon":"door-open","html":"\n<p>Leave early, in daylight, with a full tank, rather than late on icy or dark roads. Decide each evening with the Stay or go screen.</p>\n<h3>Time to leave (rules of thumb)</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Leave when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold, with babies or kids under 5</td>\n<td>The warm room can't stay above about 16\u00b0C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold, adults and older kids</td>\n<td>The warm room can't stay above about 13\u00b0C, or anyone shows hypothermia signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat</td>\n<td>Indoors stays above about 32\u00b0C, or anyone shows heat-exhaustion signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Water</td>\n<td>No running water and stored water will run out within 2 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical</td>\n<td>Any medicine or device can't be kept going</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire or official order</td>\n<td>Immediately; don't wait for power to return</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>Fallback locations</h3>\n<p>Agree on these now and add them to Our details, so the decision to leave is quick: when a \"Time to leave\" line is crossed, go to Fallback 1; if the outage is citywide or past 3 days, go to Fallback 2.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tier</th>\n<th>What to look for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fallback 1: nearby (15 to 30 min)</td>\n<td>Friends or family in another part of town, on a different part of the grid, ideally with gas heat or a generator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fallback 2: out of town (1 to 3 hours)</td>\n<td>Family, friends or a hotel outside the outage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backup hotel</td>\n<td>A family room booked by phone before you drive; hotels in the outage area fill fast or lose power too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public option, daytime</td>\n<td>Warming or cooling center at a library or rec center, as announced on local radio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public option, overnight</td>\n<td>City or county shelter; call 211 (or 311 in Austin) for locations and rides</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>How to leave</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Text your out-of-area contact where you're going and when you expect to arrive.</li>\n<li>Grab the go-bags, the medicine kit, documents and chargers.</li>\n<li>Check car seats are in the car; load water, snacks, blankets and the kids' comfort items.</li>\n<li>Pack still-cold fridge and freezer food in a cooler.</li>\n<li>In a freeze: shut off your main water valve and open the faucets to drain the lines.</li>\n<li>Unplug appliances, lock up, and tell a neighbor (or your leasing office) you've left. Don't leave a note on the door saying home is empty.</li>\n<li>Drive in daylight, on a full tank, and check the radio for road closures.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Go-bag (one per adult, plus a kid bag)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>3 days of water and no-cook food, plus kids' snacks and milk boxes</li>\n<li>Diapers, wipes, change of clothes per person, warm layers, rain gear</li>\n<li>Medications, medication list, first-aid kit, glasses</li>\n<li>Phone chargers, power bank, small battery radio, headlamp, spare batteries</li>\n<li>Cash in small bills (card readers and ATMs fail without power)</li>\n<li>Copies of IDs, insurance cards, birth certificates, a printed copy of this plan</li>\n<li>Comfort item and a small activity for each child</li>\n<li>Pet food, leash and carrier, if you have pets</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Safety at home and on the road</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Downed power line: stay at least 35 ft away, assume it's live, call 911 and your electric company.</li>\n<li>Dark traffic signal: treat the intersection as an all-way stop.</li>\n<li>Flashlights and headlamps, not candles. If you must use candles, an adult stays in the room and they sit in a sturdy holder away from kids, curtains and bedding.</li>\n<li>Smoke alarms and CO alarms with fresh batteries in every sleeping area.</li>\n<li>Lock doors and keep a light visible at night; dark neighborhoods see more break-ins.</li>\n<li>Scams spike after disasters: electric companies never demand payment by phone to restore service.</li>\n<li>When power returns: plug appliances back one at a time over several minutes; check food temperatures before eating.</li>\n<li>Fire or smoke: leave immediately with the kids, close doors behind you, never use an elevator. Keep a headlamp and shoes by the bed.</li>\n<li>Burst pipe or leak: shut your main water valve (find it now).</li>\n</ul>","text":"Leave early, in daylight, with a full tank, rather than late on icy or dark roads. Decide each evening with the Stay or go screen.\nTime to leave (rules of thumb)\nSituation\nLeave when\nCold, with babies or kids under 5\nThe warm room can't stay above about 16\u00b0C\nCold, adults and older kids\nThe warm room can't stay above about 13\u00b0C, or anyone shows hypothermia signs\nHeat\nIndoors stays above about 32\u00b0C, or anyone shows heat-exhaustion signs\nWater\nNo running water and stored water will run out within 2 days\nMedical\nAny medicine or device can't be kept going\nFire or official order\nImmediately; don't wait for power to return\nFallback locations\nAgree on these now and add them to Our details, so the decision to leave is quick: when a \"Time to leave\" line is crossed, go to Fallback 1; if the outage is citywide or past 3 days, go to Fallback 2.\nTier\nWhat to look for\nFallback 1: nearby (15 to 30 min)\nFriends or family in another part of town, on a different part of the grid, ideally with gas heat or a generator\nFallback 2: out of town (1 to 3 hours)\nFamily, friends or a hotel outside the outage\nBackup hotel\nA family room booked by phone before you drive; hotels in the outage area fill fast or lose power too\nPublic option, daytime\nWarming or cooling center at a library or rec center, as announced on local radio\nPublic option, overnight\nCity or county shelter; call 211 (or 311 in Austin) for locations and rides\nHow to leave\nText your out-of-area contact where you're going and when you expect to arrive.\nGrab the go-bags, the medicine kit, documents and chargers.\nCheck car seats are in the car; load water, snacks, blankets and the kids' comfort items.\nPack still-cold fridge and freezer food in a cooler.\nIn a freeze: shut off your main water valve and open the faucets to drain the lines.\nUnplug appliances, lock up, and tell a neighbor (or your leasing office) you've left. Don't leave a note on the door saying home is empty.\nDrive in daylight, on a full tank, and check the radio for road closures.\nGo-bag (one per adult, plus a kid bag)\n3 days of water and no-cook food, plus kids' snacks and milk boxes\nDiapers, wipes, change of clothes per person, warm layers, rain gear\nMedications, medication list, first-aid kit, glasses\nPhone chargers, power bank, small battery radio, headlamp, spare batteries\nCash in small bills (card readers and ATMs fail without power)\nCopies of IDs, insurance cards, birth certificates, a printed copy of this plan\nComfort item and a small activity for each child\nPet food, leash and carrier, if you have pets\nSafety at home and on the road\nDowned power line: stay at least 35 ft away, assume it's live, call 911 and your electric company.\nDark traffic signal: treat the intersection as an all-way stop.\nFlashlights and headlamps, not candles. If you must use candles, an adult stays in the room and they sit in a sturdy holder away from kids, curtains and bedding.\nSmoke alarms and CO alarms with fresh batteries in every sleeping area.\nLock doors and keep a light visible at night; dark neighborhoods see more break-ins.\nScams spike after disasters: electric companies never demand payment by phone to restore service.\nWhen power returns: plug appliances back one at a time over several minutes; check food temperatures before eating.\nFire or smoke: leave immediately with the kids, close doors behind you, never use an elevator. Keep a headlamp and shoes by the bed.\nBurst pipe or leak: shut your main water valve (find it now)."},{"id":"supplies-and-prep-this-week","title":"Supplies and prep this week","icon":"calculator","html":"\n<p>The tasks below take one weekend and cover most of what makes an outage dangerous. The Supplies screen sizes everything for your family.</p>\n<h3>Do this week</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Download offline maps: in Google Maps, tap your profile, Offline maps, Select your own map, and cover your area.</li>\n<li>Register at WarnCentralTexas.org for alerts at your home address, and sign up for your electric company's outage texts.</li>\n<li>Buy a battery carbon monoxide alarm for the sleeping room and test all smoke alarms.</li>\n<li>Buy a battery or hand-crank AM/FM/NOAA weather radio.</li>\n<li>Find your main water shutoff and breaker panel; label the breaker for the water heater.</li>\n<li>Fill in Our details in this app on every adult's phone, and put contact cards in wallets and backpacks.</li>\n<li>Keep the car's tank at least half full and $200 or more in small bills at home.</li>\n<li>Check your insurance (homeowner's or renter's): what's covered if food spoils or a pipe bursts.</li>\n<li>Walk your way out of home at night with a headlamp, carrying the youngest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Supply checklist (per person, 14 days)</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Amount</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Drinking water</td>\n<td>14 gallons</td>\n<td>Plus extra for kids' cups and pets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No-cook food</td>\n<td>28 to 42 meals</td>\n<td>Canned protein, beans, peanut butter, crackers, cereal, dried fruit, nuts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kids' food and milk boxes</td>\n<td>2 weeks</td>\n<td>Foods they already eat, spill-proof cups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diapers or pull-ups and wipes</td>\n<td>2 weeks</td>\n<td>Roughly 6 a day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prescriptions</td>\n<td>2 weeks minimum</td>\n<td>Plus a written medication list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warm layers</td>\n<td>1 set</td>\n<td>Thermal base layer, fleece, hat, gloves, wool socks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleeping warmth</td>\n<td>1 sleeping bag or 2 heavy blankets</td>\n<td>Plus fleece pajamas and a hat for each child</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light</td>\n<td>1 headlamp</td>\n<td>Plus 2 lanterns for the household and spare batteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power</td>\n<td>1 power bank per adult</td>\n<td>Plus a power station and solar panel for longer outages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hygiene</td>\n<td>1 kit</td>\n<td>Toilet paper, hand sanitizer, wet wipes, heavy trash bags, bleach (unscented)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cooking</td>\n<td>Household</td>\n<td>Propane camp stove (outdoor use), 2 spare cylinders, manual can opener, matches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash</td>\n<td>Household</td>\n<td>Small bills</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>Keeping the kit ready</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Every month</td>\n<td>Power banks and power station charged; flashlights work; car tank at least half full</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spring and fall (when the clocks change)</td>\n<td>Smoke and CO alarm batteries; medicine expiration dates; swap kids' spare clothes for their current size; rotate stored water you filled yourself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every fall, before freeze season</td>\n<td>Re-check phone numbers and fallback contacts; refresh the 3-day menu with foods the kids eat now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every spring, before storm and heat season</td>\n<td>Fans and cooling items ready; update the go-bags; review flood and tornado steps with the kids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After every use</td>\n<td>Replace what you used within a week</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources: American Red Cross, CDC, Ready.gov, Ready Central Texas and the electric companies' own outage pages. Checked October 2026. Huddle Ready is a planning aid, not medical advice. In an emergency, call 911.</p>","text":"The tasks below take one weekend and cover most of what makes an outage dangerous. The Supplies screen sizes everything for your family.\nDo this week\nDownload offline maps: in Google Maps, tap your profile, Offline maps, Select your own map, and cover your area.\nRegister at WarnCentralTexas.org for alerts at your home address, and sign up for your electric company's outage texts.\nBuy a battery carbon monoxide alarm for the sleeping room and test all smoke alarms.\nBuy a battery or hand-crank AM/FM/NOAA weather radio.\nFind your main water shutoff and breaker panel; label the breaker for the water heater.\nFill in Our details in this app on every adult's phone, and put contact cards in wallets and backpacks.\nKeep the car's tank at least half full and $200 or more in small bills at home.\nCheck your insurance (homeowner's or renter's): what's covered if food spoils or a pipe bursts.\nWalk your way out of home at night with a headlamp, carrying the youngest.\nSupply checklist (per person, 14 days)\nCategory\nAmount\nNotes\nDrinking water\n14 gallons\nPlus extra for kids' cups and pets\nNo-cook food\n28 to 42 meals\nCanned protein, beans, peanut butter, crackers, cereal, dried fruit, nuts\nKids' food and milk boxes\n2 weeks\nFoods they already eat, spill-proof cups\nDiapers or pull-ups and wipes\n2 weeks\nRoughly 6 a day\nPrescriptions\n2 weeks minimum\nPlus a written medication list\nWarm layers\n1 set\nThermal base layer, fleece, hat, gloves, wool socks\nSleeping warmth\n1 sleeping bag or 2 heavy blankets\nPlus fleece pajamas and a hat for each child\nLight\n1 headlamp\nPlus 2 lanterns for the household and spare batteries\nPower\n1 power bank per adult\nPlus a power station and solar panel for longer outages\nHygiene\n1 kit\nToilet paper, hand sanitizer, wet wipes, heavy trash bags, bleach (unscented)\nCooking\nHousehold\nPropane camp stove (outdoor use), 2 spare cylinders, manual can opener, matches\nCash\nHousehold\nSmall bills\nKeeping the kit ready\nWhen\nCheck\nEvery month\nPower banks and power station charged; flashlights work; car tank at least half full\nSpring and fall (when the clocks change)\nSmoke and CO alarm batteries; medicine expiration dates; swap kids' spare clothes for their current size; rotate stored water you filled yourself\nEvery fall, before freeze season\nRe-check phone numbers and fallback contacts; refresh the 3-day menu with foods the kids eat now\nEvery spring, before storm and heat season\nFans and cooling items ready; update the go-bags; review flood and tornado steps with the kids\nAfter every use\nReplace what you used within a week\nSources: American Red Cross, CDC, Ready.gov, Ready Central Texas and the electric companies' own outage pages. Checked October 2026. Huddle Ready is a planning aid, not medical advice. In an emergency, call 911."}];